This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Furthermore, we have demonstrated that the obtained solution has the maximal growth property when combined with a unique radial symmetry property. Lastly, we have extended our findings to the atypical case where $\lambda$ assumes a different value. Additionally, we have explored a variety of perturbed cases and established the existence of a normalized ground state solution for the perturbed equation via a similar approach. To validate our results, we have compared the critical points of several functionals with the normalized ground state solution we obtained. Ultimately, we have derived several key estimates and demonstrated the uniqueness of the obtained solution. This work has broadened the scope of the Choquard type equation and has opened up new avenues for research in this area. Some of the technical lemmas and propositions used in this paper are presented in the appendix for reference. To the best of our knowledge, the findings presented in this paper are the first of their kind to be reported. This research was partially supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11671017) and the Natural Science Foundation of Jiangsu Province (Grant No. 180722). This study was also partially funded by the Jiangsu Provincial Government. This work was completed at Jiangsu University. This paper is available online at the indicated link. This is the authors' version of the article, which has been peer-reviewed and accepted for publication in the journal Journal of Contemporary Mathematics, Volume 2022, Article  number 113. This is an open-access article, distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative

